// Values that arrive from outside the program: what a query, an API call or a
// database row is to the program *before* it has answered.
//
// `remote.Value<T>` is the three-state box every data adapter returns. A plain
// async call cannot describe the states a fetch passes through: the compiler's
// async lowering seeds a reactive async computed with the zero value of its
// type, so a fetch in flight, a fetch that failed, and a fetch that legitimately
// returned `0` are the same value. A `Value` separates them by holding three
// independent facts instead of one:
//
//   - the last value that arrived, if one ever did
//   - how the most recent attempt failed, if it did
//   - whether an attempt is in flight right now
//
// Every accessor answers on every box, including one nothing has ever fetched:
// its zero is the idle box — no value, no failure, nothing in flight — so
// `value()` on it is null rather than a fault. That is the reason the box wraps
// an `option<T>` instead of a `T`: with the absence carried in the type, no
// accessor has a case it cannot answer, and no reader needs a guard before
// asking.
//
// The three are independent on purpose, because the interesting states are the
// combinations. A stale value beside a failure is a refresh that did not land,
// and the honest thing to render is the old value with a warning — not a
// spinner, and not an empty screen. Collapsing them into one status enum is what
// makes that case unexpressible, which is why there is no such enum, and why
// `refresh()` keeps the value it has until new data replaces it.
//
// A box that fetches comes from a `#[query]` function and from nothing else:
//
//     #[query]
//     func users(page int) remote.Value<list<User>> {
//         return api.listUsers(page)
//     }
//
// The mark is what says this function's answer is keyed and cached — one box per
// argument tuple, re-fetched when an argument changes, shared by every part of
// the program asking the same question. An *unmarked* function returning a
// `Value` is an ordinary function that happens to return one.
//
// A bare `T` is not a `Value<T>` and does not become one on its own. This is
// where the type parts company with `option<T>`, which promotes in one
// direction: an option is "maybe a `T`" and promoting one loses nothing, while a
// `Value` carries fetch state that no plain value implies, and a silent
// promotion would invent a settled box wherever two types happened to line up.
// `of`, `failedWith` and `pending` construct the three states explicitly — for
// an adapter that already holds its answer, and for a test that needs to render
// one branch.
//
// The adapters are packages of their own — `sngl:remote/graphql`,
// `sngl:remote/sql` — and a program pays for one by importing it. What lives
// here is only the vocabulary they all return.
